## Step 5 — Final Reflection

Learning Codex was difficult at first because I had to learn how specific my instructions needed to be. Since Codex was connected directly to Roblox Studio, I could describe what I wanted and have it build or change things in the game, but it did not always understand exactly what I meant on the first try. The strategy that helped me adapt the fastest was breaking the game into smaller systems and giving Codex detailed rules for each one. For example, instead of simply asking it to make an animal spawning system, I could tell it that the forest should have a maximum of 80 critters, that animals should have different rarity levels, and that each animal should have a random weight. This worked because Codex had much clearer information to work with and I could test each system before moving on. At the beginning I felt a little overwhelmed because I was trying to understand both the tool and how I wanted the game to work at the same time. By the end, I felt much more confident because I had created several connected systems and completed a working questline. I learned that I work best with new tools when I can experiment with them directly and see immediate results. I also learned that using AI does not remove the need for problem solving because I still had to design the game, decide how the systems should work, test everything, and correct problems. In the future, I would use the same process with a new tool by starting with a clear goal, breaking the problem into smaller parts, testing often, and improving my instructions based on the results.
